| Layer | Name               | What it does                                            | Examples                         |
| ----: | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| **4** | **Application**    | Provides network services to applications and users     | HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SSH, FTP, SMTP |
| **3** | **Transport**      | Provides end-to-end communication between devices       | **TCP**, UDP                     |
| **2** | **Internet**       | Handles logical addressing and routing between networks | **IP, ICMP, IPsec**              |
| **1** | **Network Access** | Handles communication over the physical/local network   | Ethernet, Wi-Fi, ARP             |

# An easy way to remember it:
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ 4. APPLICATION               │
│    HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SSH     │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ 3. TRANSPORT                 │
│    TCP, UDP                  │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ 2. INTERNET                  │
│    IP, ICMP                  │
├──────────────────────────────┤
│ 1. NETWORK ACCESS            │
│    Ethernet, Wi-Fi, ARP      │
└──────────────────────────────┘

# How datamoves through layers:
You type:
https://example.com
        ↓
APPLICATION
HTTPS / HTTP
        ↓
TRANSPORT
TCP
        ↓
INTERNET
IP
        ↓
NETWORK ACCESS
Ethernet / Wi-Fi
        ↓
     Network
        ↓
Destination server

# The computer receives it in the opposite direction:
Network Access
      ↓
Internet
      ↓
Transport
      ↓
Application
      ↓
Web application

# how this relates to the 7-layer OSI model:
| TCP/IP             | OSI equivalent |
| ------------------ | -------------- |
| **Application**    | 7 Application  |
|                    | 6 Presentation |
|                    | 5 Session      |
| **Transport**      | 4 Transport    |
| **Internet**       | 3 Network      |
| **Network Access** | 2 Data Link    |
|                    | 1 Physical     |

# memorize this sequence:
Application → Transport → Internet → Network Access

# And remember the major protocols:
HTTP/HTTPS → TCP/UDP → IP → Ethernet/Wi-Fi
